BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260922T220000Z
DTEND:20260922T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20260922T000000Z
SUMMARY:SALAMANDERS!
DESCRIPTION:SALAMANDERS! \n\nWho? What? Where? When? Why? \n\n 
 \n\n\n\nHellbenders\, water dogs\, mudpuppies\, snot otters\, lasagna 
 lizards\, devil dogs\, and mud devils... \n\nCovered in mucus... \n\nLiving 
 for decades... \n\nLungless and breathing through their skin... \n\nBorn 
 from fire... \n\n \n\n\n\nWho is the state salamander? What is essential to 
 salamanders? Where can you find the biggest salamander in North Carolina? 
 When can you see salamanders? Why is North Carolina the Salamander Capital 
 of the World?  \n\n \n\n\n\nJoin Jeff Hall\, the Statewide Herpetologist 
 and NC Partners in Amphibian and Reptile Conservation program lead for the 
 North Carolina Wildlife Resource Commission\, to learn more about these 
 native hellbenders\, mudpuppies\, and water dogs! \n\n\n\nHired in 2007 by 
 the North Carolina Wildlife Resources Commission\, Jeff Hall is the 
 Statewide Herpetologist and NC Partners in Amphibian and Reptile 
 Conservation program lead. Through his work with NCPARC\, Jeff works with 
 landowners to promote habitat management that benefits reptiles and 
 amphibians as well as other wildlife species. He coordinates the North 
 Carolina chapter of PARC helping to bring public and private partners 
 together to further conservation efforts for reptiles and amphibians. Jeff 
 also participates in field work on a variety of projects including rare 
 amphibian monitoring and habitat restoration\, upland snake conservation\, 
 and Project Bog Turtle. In addition\, Hall manages the Calling Amphibian 
 Survey Program (CASP) which is designed to monitor long-term trends of frog 
 and toad populations across the state. Jeff and his wife Shannon make their 
 home in Greenville\, NC. \n\n\n\nTuesday September 22nd starting at 6 p.m. 
 in the Durham County Main Library auditorium located at 300 North Roxboro 
 Street in Durham.       \n\nParking is available in the library parking lot 
 or at the North Queen Street Garage located at 115 North Queen Street which 
 is behind the apartments directly across the street from the library. 
 Parking at the garage is free. Please bring your parking ticket to a 
 service point for validation.         \n\n  \n\nRegistration is appreciated 
 to receive an email reminder\, cancellation link\, and program updates. 
 Seating is first come first served. Registration does not hold or guarantee 
 a seat.       \n\nThere will be no live animals or insects at this event.   
   \n\nQuestions? Email mkoslofsky@dconc.gov  \n\nNote: If you have any 
 accessibility questions or accommodation needs\, please contact me at 
 mkoslofsky@dconc.gov or call 919-560-9311. Please make all requests three 
 business days prior to the event to ensure I can provide the best 
 accommodations possible!\n\n  \n\nImage description: The Marbled 
 salamander\, Ambystoma opacum\, is a small dark grey and black salamander 
 that is native to North Carolina.  \n\nPhoto credit: Brian Gratwicke
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Megan Koslofsky":MAILTO:mkoslofsky@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:
UID:LibCal-14288-15425310
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/15425310
END:VEVENT
