BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260925T230000Z
DTEND:20260926T003000Z
DTSTAMP:20260925T000000Z
SUMMARY:Ethics of Now with Erica Armstrong Dunbar
DESCRIPTION:\n\nWhat does history look like through the eyes of those whose 
 stories are rarely told?\n\nThrough her historical writing and work on the 
 HBO series “The Gilded Age\,” Erica Armstrong Dunbar offers us 
 portraits of African Americans in the nineteenth century\, people as 
 complex and compelling as the times in which they lived. One is the 
 remarkable Ona Judge\, who escaped from slavery — and her former owners\, 
 George and Martha Washington — when she was twenty years old. Though she 
 lived the rest of her life as a fugitive\, Judge was “Never Caught” — 
 despite the Washingtons’ repeated attempts to return her to slavery. 
 \n\nOn the 250th anniversary of 1776\, Dunbar invites us to consider how a 
 fuller narrative of our past enriches the American story — and our 
 present and future as a nation e pluribus unum\, or “out of many\, 
 one.”\n\n \nEvent Details:\n\nThis event takes place at the Durham County 
 Main Library. Parking is available onsite. A reception and book signing 
 will take place after the hour-long event\, and copies of Dunbar’s books 
 “Never Caught: The Washingtons’ Relentless Pursuit of Their Runaway 
 Slave\, Ona Judge” and “She Came to Slay: The Life and Times of Harriet 
 Tubman” will be available for purchase\, courtesy of Parker & Otis.\n\nIf 
 you have questions about this event\, please contact Hillary Train at 
 hillary.train@duke.edu.\n\n \nAdditional Information About the Speaker and 
 Event Sponsors: \n\nErica Armstrong Dunbar is a historian\, professor of 
 African American Studies at Emory University\, and main historical 
 consultant and co-executive producer for HBO’s “The Gilded Age.” Her 
 work shines a light on racial injustice\, slavery\, and gender inequality. 
 She is the author of “Never Caught: The Washingtons’ Relentless Pursuit 
 of Their Runaway Slave\, Ona Judge\,” which was a finalist for the 2017 
 National Book Award for Nonfiction and received the 2018 Frederick Douglass 
 Book Award. \n\nA startling and eye-opening look into America’s First 
 Family\, “Never Caught” tells the story of Ona Judge\, George and 
 Martha Washington’s runaway slave who risked it all to escape the 
 nation’s capital and reach freedom. Dunbar gives readers a glimpse into 
 the life of a little-known but powerful figure in American history and the 
 ensuing manhunt led by George Washington\, who attempted to use his 
 political and personal contacts to recapture his property.\n\n“Never 
 Caught” is the 2026 Duke Common Experience reading. It is integrated with 
 We The People\, a university-wide initiative led by Duke Arts\, which marks 
 the 250th anniversary of the signing of the Declaration of Independence. 
 \n\nAdditional sponsors for Dunbar’s visit include Duke Libraries\, the 
 Office of Undergraduate Education’s Duke Common Experience Program\, and 
 the Duke Department of History.
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Larkin Coffey":MAILTO:lcoffey@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Author Visit, Humanities
UID:LibCal-14288-16756497
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/16756497
END:VEVENT
