SUMMARY:The Food We Flush: Using Wastewater to Understand What Durham Eats
DESCRIPTION:The project “Wastewater-Based Epidemiology for 
 Community-Level Dietary Assessment” is currently underway in Durham\, led 
 by a team of researcher from Duke in partnership with the City of Durham. 
 The project uses a novel approach to measure leftover food DNA in 
 wastewater samples to better understand neighborhood-level dietary patterns 
 across Durham\, with potential for future expansion beyond the city. The 
 team is also examining how neighborhood food environments\, reflected 
 through wastewater-based dietary patterns\, relate to health outcomes. 
 Ultimately\, the project aims to support local health programs\, food 
 access efforts\, and broader understanding of the relationship between 
 nutrition and wellness.\n\nResearchers will be present about about this 
 project\, answer questions about how food DNA is extracted from 
 wastewater\, and provide an opportunity for community comments on what 
 should be done with this data.\n\nParking is available in the library lot 
 or at the North Queen Street Garage which is locate at 115 North Queen 
 Street which is located directly across the street from the library. 
 Parking is free in the garage. Bring your parking ticket to a library 
 service point for validation. \n\nQuestions? Email Jimmy McKinnell: 
 jmckinnell@dconc.gov
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="James McKinnell":MAILTO:jmckinnell@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:
UID:LibCal-14288-17080309
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/17080309
END:VEVENT
