DTSTAMP:20261002T000000Z
SUMMARY:Press Play Durham
DESCRIPTION:Grab your tickets at this link\n\nVideo Consortium is back on 
 the road with their fifth Press Play Tour stop in Durham\, NC\, taking 
 place Friday October 2 at the Durham County Main Library! \n\nThis Press 
 Play will feature a panel\, breakout conversations\, networking\, live 
 music and free local bites from Boricua Soul. Anyone interested in 
 nonfiction video is welcome! Whether you’re a veteran video journalist or 
 a student\, Press Play is your chance to gain valuable new connections\, 
 tools\, and insight into the industry. \n\nFeatured screenings: PBS North 
 Carolina’s Homegrown History\, as well as Theory of Change\, a story 
 about organizing and community. \n\nGrab your tickets at this link\, and 
 make sure to spread the word to friends in the area!\n\n\n\n\n	3:00 pm: 
 check-in table opens near the 2nd floor entrance of Durham County Main 
 Library\n	 \n	4:00 pm: intro and opening panel convo \n	\n	\n		Who do you 
 trust for information and why? \n		In this opening conversation\, we'll 
 explore the dynamics between local nonfiction storytelling and the broader 
 community. Our panel will discuss where folks get their information from\, 
 and why they trust these sources instead of others. What is the 
 responsibility of the storytellers and how do they engage with audiences? 
 And how can audiences approach where they themselves source 
 information?\n		\n			Panelists: Jameka Autry\, Sarah Michels\, Gabriel 
 Cruz\n			Moderator: Natalie Bullock Brown\n			 \n		\n		\n	\n	\n	5:00 pm: 
 community breakout conversations \n	\n	\n		Topics to include: 
 \n		\n			critical thinking & parsing through so much media \n			local 
 stories with big impact — what makes a story take off and engage with 
 people\n			building trust with participants & why extractive journalism 
 fails\n			capturing footage — from the technical craft of getting the 
 shot\, to how authentication tools are helping protect journalism \n			how 
 to engage & gather locally to enhance media literacy \n			 
 \n		\n		\n	\n	\n	5:45 pm: break!\n	 \n	6:00 pm: local shorts 
 screening\n	\n		The Warrior at Valley Forge They Didn't Teach You About 
 (dir. Sandra Katharine Davidson)\n		Theory of Change (dir. Matt Durning) 
 \n		screening Q&A featuring members of the filmmaking team: Matt Durning\, 
 Carey Kirk\, Shikara Fraser & Nicole Arrington\n		 \n	\n	\n	7:15 pm: food 
 from Boricua Soul and music (DJ!) \n	 \n	8:00 pm: wrap and head to the 
 afterparty at Eve Botanical Lounge\, hosted by DAWG!\n\n\nGrab your tickets 
 at this link\n\n\n	\n	\n	Powered by Knight Foundation\, Press Play Durham 
 is made possible by community partners Carolina Public Press\, PBS NC\, 
 Durham County Library\, Southern Documentary Fund\, NC Local News\, Look 
 Different\, The Assembly\, INDY Week\, The Documentary Accountability 
 Working Group\, Sony\, Rode\, and Kate B. Reynolds Charitable 
 Trust.\n	\n\n\n\nInformation on Entering Main Library\n\nParking is 
 available in the library parking lot or at the North Queen Street Garage 
 located at 115 North Queen Street which is behind the apartments directly 
 across the street from the library.
