SUMMARY:Japanese Paper Arts: Chiri-e (Torn Paper Art)
DESCRIPTION:Designed for parents and children to explore paper arts 
 together\, but all are welcomed! This hands-on program introduces Chigiri-e 
 (Torn Paper Art)! In this hands-on class\, participants will tear paper (no 
 scissors) and glue pieces to make a picture with soft edges. This art form 
 comes from Japanese washi paper craft.\n\n\n\nFriday\, October 9\, 
 2026\n\n11:30 a.m.-12:30 p.m.\n\nMaker Lab at Durham County Main Library 
 (300 N. Roxboro St.\, Durham\, NC)\n\nRegistration preferred 
 \n\n\n\nAdditionally\, the JOI teacher will introduce Japanese sports day 
 events and school life during the class.\n\nThis is Session 2 of 2 Japanese 
 paper art classes offered this morning. You are welcome to attend 1 or both 
 sessions! If you would also like to join the Chiri-e (Torn Paper Art) class 
 from 11:30 AM – 12:30 PM\, click here to view and register for Session 
 1.\n\n\nPresented in partnership with the Japan-America Society\, Japan 
 Outreach Initiative (JOY) Program\, UNC Wilmington\, Tenma Tabi Japan\, and 
 Durham County Library Humanities.\n\n \n\n-\n\n\nInformation on Entering 
 Main Library\n\nParking is available in the library parking lot or at the 
 North Queen Street Garage located at 115 North Queen Street which is behind 
 the apartments directly across the street from the library.
LOCATION:MakerLab\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Celeste Holtzmann":MAILTO:choltzmann@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Arts & Crafts, Classes & Lectures, Humanities
