SUMMARY:Open Sew with Connecting with Threads
DESCRIPTION:This class is geared towards tweens and teens who already know 
 how to sew and/or have completed 12 weeks of our Learn To Sew program.  In 
 this program\, they will learn how to complete intermediate level projects 
 from start to finish as they expand their knowledge from basic sewing 
 skills to creating complete projects from a pattern. They will also be 
 making a project to give to someone in our community--perhaps a dog blanket 
 or a quilt for a baby in the NICU.\n\nThe six programs are scheduled for: 
 August 29\, Sept 12\, Sept. 26\, Oct. 10\, Oct. 17\, and Oct. 24. Many 
 projects will take more than one session to complete\, so be sure to sign 
 up for multiple sessions. For tweens (ages 9 and up) and teens Registration 
 is required! You must register individually for each session you plan to 
 attend.
LOCATION:Southwest Regional - Children's Programming Room\, Southwest Regional Library (3605 Shannon Rd)
ORGANIZER;CN="Caroline P.":MAILTO:cbpeterson@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Arts & Crafts
UID:LibCal-14288-17349110
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/17349110
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20261010T173000Z
DTEND:20261010T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20261010T000000Z
SUMMARY:Everything You Ever Wanted to Know About Writing and Publishing
DESCRIPTION:Got a story to tell? Ever wondered what it would take to get 
 published? Join Triangle Sisters in Crime at Main Library for a panel 
 discussion on how to make it happen! \n\nA panel of published author 
 members of the Triangle Sisters in Crime will share insider tips 
 on:\n\n\n\n1) how to write the best story possible.\n\n2) grabbing the 
 attention of agents\, editors\, and readers. \n\n3) choosing the best 
 publishing option for your work\, including self-publishing\, small press\, 
 and traditional publishing. \n\n\n\nAttendees will learn tips for success 
 and survival in this ever-changing industry. The perfect program for 
 writers in any genre. Bring your questions\, meet the authors\, and go home 
 with lots of helpful information and some entertaining 
 books!\n\n\n\nPanelists will include: Ashley-Ruth Bernier\, Melissa 
 Bourbon/Ivy Cassidy/Winnie Archer\, Nora Gaskin\, Victoria Gilbert\, Ruben 
 Gonzales\, Diane Kelly\, G.L.T. Lyons\, Michael Mayo\, P.M. Raymond\, and 
 Marlie Wasserman. \n\nThis event will also be streamed on Zoom\, virtual 
 attendees may register here. \n\n\n\n\n\nAbout the 
 authors:\n\n\n\nAshley-Ruth Bernier\, Award-winning author Ashley-Ruth M. 
 Bernier writes short mystery and crime stories set in her home island of 
 St. Thomas\, and her debut novel\, THE BUSH TEA MURDER\, was released in 
 April 2026. \n\n\n\nMelissa Bourbon/Ivy Cassidy/Winnie Archer is a national 
 bestselling author of more than thirty-five novels\, founder of WriterSpark 
 Academy and the Bourbon & Books YouTube channel\, a staff member at Pocket 
 MFA\, and the creator of one of Writer’s Digest’s 101 Best Websites for 
 Writers.\n\n\n\nNora Gaskin lives and writes in Chatham County\, inspired 
 by her native landscape\, her husband\, and at least one dog-in-residence. 
 \n\n\n\nVictoria Gilbert is a retired librarian who lives in the North 
 Carolina Triad and is the author of four cozy and traditional mystery 
 series -- the Blue Ridge Library Mystery series\, the Booklover's B&B 
 series\, the Hunter and Clewe mystery series\, and the Campus Sleuth 
 mystery series. \n\n\n\nRuben Gonzales usually writes in the mystery genre 
 but his latest book to be published by Histria Books\, is historical 
 fiction about the Mexican revolution and follows the exciting exploits of a 
 woman soldier whose character is based on his great 
 grandmother.\n\n\n\nDiane Kelly writes funny mysteries featuring feisty 
 females with furry sidekicks\, including the Death and Taxes series\, Paw 
 Enforcement K-9 series\, House Flipper mystery series\, Busted motorcycle 
 cop series\, Southern Homebrew moonshine series\, and Mountain Lodge 
 Mysteries.\n\n\n\nG.L.T. Lyons is the author and illustrator of the Library 
 of Hereafter magical realism series and the Destiny Bay mystery series 
 under the pen name\, Gina Lea.\n\n\n\nMichael Mayo is the author of the 
 Jimmy Quinn historical suspense novels\, and he has written extensively 
 about film and popular culture in books\, newspapers and other 
 media.\n\n\n\nP.M. Raymond is an award-winning author and aspiring 
 screenwriter from New Orleans whose work spans horror\, crime\, and 
 Southern Gothic fiction.\n\n\n\nMarlie Wasserman loves writing historical 
 crime fiction from her desk in Chapel Hill\, North Carolina.\n\n\n\n
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Larkin Coffey":MAILTO:lcoffey@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:
UID:LibCal-14288-15568705
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/15568705
END:VEVENT
